{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And he released him.</b> The angel — after he was circumcised, <b>then</b> she understood that it was because of the circumcision, and <b>she said: a bridegroom of blood, for the circumcisions.</b> What made Moses a bridegroom of blood — that was the circumcisions, on account of the circumcision of her son; therefore she did not say 'for the circumcisions' until after 'and he released him.' Another interpretation: <b>And she said</b> about her son — 'for a bridegroom of blood you are to me' — she called her son a bridegroom of blood on the day of his circumcision, on account of the commandment; and so the meaning is, 'You, my son — a <b>bridegroom of blood you are to me</b>,' for the angel is shedding the blood of my husband. <b>A bridegroom of blood for the circumcisions.</b> What I called my son 'a bridegroom of blood' — that was for the circumcisions. And all the time that he was in his father-in-law's house he was not punished, because it was not in his power to do so on account of his father-in-law who prevented him, as is stated in chapter HaGozeil: the angel sought to kill him at the inn on account of his firstborn son, whom he had not yet circumcised; and this matter caused his grandson to become an idolater. Therefore the angel hastened to kill Moses immediately, 'and Tziporah took a flint,' for through her was the obstruction. Nevertheless, the reasoning of most of the world inclines to the view that it was about her young son — the younger of her two sons — that she said, 'for a bridegroom of blood you are to me'; but one does not refute the aggadah. And if you ask: earlier, regarding 'and the priest of Midian,' Rashi explained that he separated from idolatry. Rather, one may say: not specifically from idolatry, but rather that he not be circumcised and be as a ger toshav." "<b>For a bridegroom of blood you are to me.</b> Because Rashi explained: 'blood' — as in 'one who sheds blood,' like 'go out, man of blood.' And one must rearrange the verse: 'for blood — a bridegroom you are to me,' that is, 'my husband's killer you are to me.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "פרש״‎י is expanded as Rashi. ד״‎א resolved as 'another interpretation' (דבר אחר). מ״‎מ resolved as 'nevertheless' (מכל מקום). נ״‎ל resolved as 'one may say' (ייתכן לומר / נראה לי)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:56:21.371747102Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}